---
import BaseLayout from '@platform/core/src/layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import { getSiteConfig, getPersona } from '@platform/core/src/lib/config'
const cfg = getSiteConfig()
const persona = getPersona()
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`How We Research — ${cfg.site.brand_name}`}
  description={`Our editorial methodology: how ${persona.name_used} researches and evaluates ${cfg.site.niche} products.`}
>
  <div class="container" style="padding-top:2rem; padding-bottom:4rem; max-width:720px;">
    <h1>How We Research {cfg.site.niche_label ?? cfg.site.niche} Products</h1>
    <p>{persona.name_used}'s personal experience navigating hearing healthcare decisions shapes what we look for — but every recommendation on this site is built on research and verified sourcing, not personal product testing.</p>

    <h2>What we evaluate</h2>
    <p>For each hearing aid or assistive device: technology type (RIC, BTE, ITE, OTC), amplification range and audiogram fit, connectivity features, battery life as stated in manufacturer specifications, trial period and return policy, service network and warranty terms, and listed product features compared against published specifications and current retailer listings.</p>

    <h2>How we source information</h2>
    <p>We compile our guides using manufacturer specifications and current product listings, supplemented by publicly available consumer guidance (including resources from ASHA, AAA, and BHI) and published clinical and comparative studies where available. These guides are intended as shopping research; we earn a commission when you buy through our links.</p>

    <h2>What we do not do</h2>
    <p>We do not conduct audiological assessments. We do not claim personal product testing when the basis is research. We do not accept free devices in exchange for coverage. We do not reduce complex hearing health decisions to star ratings.</p>

    <h2>A note on OTC versus prescription hearing aids</h2>
    <p>Some products covered on this site are FDA-registered over-the-counter (OTC) hearing aids intended for adults with perceived mild-to-moderate hearing loss. Others are prescription devices that require fitting by a licensed audiologist. We aim to clearly distinguish between these categories. If you are unsure which is appropriate for you, consult a hearing healthcare professional before purchasing.</p>

    <h2>Update cadence</h2>
    <p>The hearing aid market changes quickly — new chipsets, OTC category expansion, and firmware updates affect recommendations. We update articles when new products launch, when specifications change materially, or when manufacturer information and current product listings indicate that earlier details are no longer accurate.</p>

    <h2>Affiliate links</h2>
    <p>Some links on this site are affiliate links. If you buy through one, we earn a commission at no extra cost to you. Our buying guides are compiled from manufacturer specifications and current product listings, not independent product testing, so please read them as shopping research rather than editorial reviews. See our <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">full affiliate disclosure</a>.</p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
